Uncomplicated superficial skin tear from minor friction to forearm
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Irregular fragile epidermal flap, Shallow capillary oozing, Minor sheer force mechanism on atrophic skin

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-042
Reason for Visit: Acute, bleeding skin tear in a patient with fragile skin, requiring urgent realignment and specialized non-adherent dressing application.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and primary caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute skin tear with a bleeding dermal flap resulting from minor friction. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to achieve hemostasis, carefully realign the viable tissue flap to preserve the skin barrier, and apply specific silicone dressings to prevent severe tissue necrosis and secondary infection before daytime clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I bumped my arm against the bed rail and the skin just peeled right back, and it keeps weeping blood.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a clear history of minor sheer force and friction against equipment 1 hour ago. This resulted in the jagged, irregular tearing of the fragile, atrophic epidermis, producing a visible skin flap (Payne-Martin classification). There is shallow bleeding consisting primarily of capillary oozing that is easily arrested with gentle pressure. The patient complains of mild to moderate pain well-localized to the disrupted tissue. The patient has pre-existing thin, parchment-like skin typical of advanced age.
Functional Status: Fully independent in ADLs, but skin fragility requires careful manual handling.
Social/Safety Concerns: Recurrent risk of minor friction injuries due to environmental hazards (bed rails, sharp furniture corners).

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/84 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable at rest, holding a light tissue over the forearm.
Physical Exam: Forearm examination reveals an irregular fragile epidermal flap with shallow capillary oozing. There is exposure of the superficial dermis without visualization of underlying subcutaneous fat, muscle, or fascia. The skin is highly atrophic (parchment-like). There is a lack of significant surrounding ecchymosis, deep hematoma, or massive tissue crush. The skin flap is viable and can be gently rolled back to re-approximate the wound margins without tension. A completely normal distal limb neurovascular examination is confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Metal bed rail noted; caregiver advised to apply padded covers.
Medication Reconciliation: Currently on low-dose aspirin for cardiovascular primary prevention.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Uncomplicated superficial skin tear to forearm - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Deep dermal laceration - Ruled out due to: Injury limited entirely to the epidermal layer without penetration into the subcutaneous fat or fascial planes.

Ischemic tissue necrosis - Ruled out due to: Viable, pink skin flap with brisk capillary refill upon realignment.

Cellulitis - Ruled out due to: Acute traumatic timeline and complete absence of spreading warmth, induration, or purulence.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Skin tears in atrophic epidermis require meticulous, immediate intervention to preserve the detached flap and utilize it as a biological dressing. Bedside assessment confirmed an uncomplicated tear without deep tissue involvement. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to gently realign the viable flap and apply a silicone contact layer, directly averting the irreversible drying and death of the skin flap that would occur if left untreated or poorly dressed overnight, effectively preventing a chronic, non-healing ulcer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Gently cleansed the wound with warmed sterile saline. Using a moistened cotton swab, the epidermal flap was carefully unrolled and realigned over the wound bed. A non-adherent silicone contact layer (e.g., Mepitel) was applied, secured with a light cohesive bandage. Arrow drawn on the bandage to indicate the direction of flap removal.
Positive Behavioral Change: When the outer bandage is eventually changed, always peel it off strictly in the direction of the drawn arrow. Peeling it backward will instantly rip the fragile healing flap off the arm, destroying the healing process and creating a painful, open wound.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None currently indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Padded covers, cohesive retention bandages.
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review in 3-5 days for a scheduled outer dressing change, leaving the primary silicone layer intact.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the local doctor or Emergency Department if the arm becomes excessively red, hot, swollen, or if the wound begins to smell foul and discharge thick yellow fluid.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Fragile skin heals slowly; the flap may still darken and slough off, requiring ongoing wound care.
Clinical Course: The oozing will stop within 24 hours. The realigned flap will act as a natural scab and slowly fuse back to the underlying tissue over 7 to 14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical attention if a large, hard, painful lump of blood forms underneath the dressing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The thin layer of torn skin is placed back over the wound like a puzzle piece to protect the raw nerves and speed up healing. The silicone dressing holds it in place without sticking to it.
Actionable Steps: Do not let the bandage get wet in the shower. Pad the sharp corners of furniture in your home. Do not peel the bandage off to look at the wound.

